nes. Non-sunny lines can be vertical lines covering whole columns, but each vertical line can cover an entire column. So we need at least as many vertical lines as the number of columns that have more than k points not covered by sunny lines. Since there are n columns, we have n-k non-sunny lines. If k ≥ 4, maybe we cannot cover all columns because some columns have many points.\n\nLet's try to formalize: For column i, there are n+1-i points. If we have k sunny lines, they can cover at most k points in column i. So the remaining points in column i are at least (n+1-i) - k. These must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical lines (cover entire column) or horizontal lines (cover one point per column) or slope -1 lines (cover one point per column). Actually a horizontal line y = c intersects column i at point (i,c) if c ≤ n+1-i. So each horizontal line can cover at most one point per column. Similarly, a slope -1 line x+y = c intersects column i at point (i, c-i) if c-i ≥ 1 and ≤ n+1-i. So each such line also covers at most one point per column. So any non-sunny line that is not vertical can cover at most one point per column. So to cover the remaining points in column i beyond those covered by sunny lines, we need enough non-sunny lines that intersect column i at those points. Since each non-sunny line can intersect column i at most once, we need at least (n+1-i) - k non-sunny lines that intersect column i. But we have only n-k non-sunny lines total. So we need (n+1-i) - k ≤ n - k for each i, i.e., n+1-i ≤ n, which is true for all i≥1. So that doesn't give a bound.\n\nBut we need to consider that each non-sunny line can intersect many columns (if it's vertical, it intersects exactly one column but covers all points in that column; if it's horizontal or slope -1, it intersects each column at most once). So we need to allocate the non-sunny lines to cover the remaining points across columns. The vertical lines are the most efficient: each vertical