er (n - 1 - k) points. We could use vertical line x=2 to cover them all, but we have only n - k - 1 non-sunny lines left. So we need to decide if we can allocate vertical lines to each column with many remaining points. But we have limited number of vertical lines.\n\nThus the column counting argument might lead to a bound on k. Let's try to derive a bound.\n\nWe have n columns i = 1,...,n. Column i has n+1-i points. Sunny lines can cover at most k points per column. So remaining points in column i: max(0, n+1-i - k). For each column i, we need to cover these remaining points using non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical lines (cover all remaining points in a column) or horizontal/slope -1 lines (cover at most one point per column). So the minimal number of non-sunny lines needed is at least the number of columns i for which remaining points > 0 (since each such column needs at least one non-sunny line, either vertical or horizontal/slope -1). Actually if remaining points > 0, we could cover them with a vertical line (one per column). So we need at least the number of columns i where n+1-i > k. That is columns i ≤ n - k (since n+1-i > k => i n+1 - k => i ≤ n - k). So there are n - k columns with more than k points. So we need at least n - k non-sunny lines to cover those columns (one per column). But we have exactly n - k non-sunny lines. So we must use exactly one non-sunny line per each of those columns, and those lines must be vertical lines (since horizontal lines cannot cover more than one point per column, but we have more than k points left, so we need vertical lines to cover all remaining points). So we must assign vertical lines to columns i = 1,...,n - k. That uses all n - k non-sunny lines. Then we have no non-sunny lines left for columns i > n - k (i.e., columns with ≤ k points). For those columns, we must rely on sunny lines to cover all points. Since each column i > n - k has at most k points, and we have k sunny lines, each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so we can cover at most k points per column. Since the column has at most k points, we can cover them all with the k sunny lines (by assigning each point to a distinct sunny line). However, we need to ensure that each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover points in many columns. So we need to assign each point in columns i > n - k to a sunny line, ensuring that each sunny line gets at most one point per column. Since there are k sunny lines, we can assign each column's points to distinct sunny lines (if the column has exactly k points, each sunny line gets one point; if fewer, some sunny lines get none). So this is possible.\n\nThus the column counting argument yields that k can be any integer from 0 to n? Wait, we derived that we need n - k vertical lines for columns i ≤ n - k. That uses all non-sunny lines. So we must have exactly n - k vertical lines, and no other non-sunny lines. So the construction must be: use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n - k, and use k sunny lines to cover the remaining points in columns n - k + 1,...,n. This seems plausible for any k ≤ n. However, we need to ensure that the points in columns i > n - k can be covered by k sunny lines. Let's examine.\n\nColumns i > n - k have at most k points each. For i = n - k + 1, column size = n+1 - (n - k + 1) = k. For i = n - k + 2, column size = k - 1, etc. So column sizes are k, k-1, ..., 1. So we have a triangular shape of size k. So we need to cover a triangular region of size k(k+1)/2 points with k sunny lines. Is that possible? For k=0, trivial. For k=1, we need to cover a column of size 1 (just point (n,1)). That's possible with a sunny line (any line through that point). For k=2, we need to cover points in columns n-1 and n: column n-1 has 2 points: (n-1,1),(n-1,2); column n has 1 point: (n,1). So total 3 points. Can we cover them with 2 sunny lines? Yes: we can use line through (n-1,1) and (n,1) (horizontal, not sunny) - not allow